From 1273771b42eebe2da96c1c0e963f239893cd205c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 08:44:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban priority: capture reads the inventory at one
 fixed head (mid-window creations join it)

Problem: `supervisor-requests --priority-operation origins|migrate` refused
about half the time on the busy default board with "canonical inventory
changed; capture a new manifest" even after #1947 (open<->terminal flips) and
#1975 (page-head order). An instrumented MacBook capture (14 pages, 55-180 s)
showed exactly one trip per refusal, always "missing from snapshot, open at
target" (t_01a1d3f04e37f0cf on 95fe, t_01a1d4087cc6545b on 99ae). New
ULID-style ids (t_01a1d4...) sort before the legacy ids (t_c21c170e...), so the
page covering a card created mid-capture was read before the card existed, and
the board creates about one card a minute.

Fix: check_interval now produces the inventory at ONE fixed head (the final
observed head) by construction. The Board pages at most 1000 rows per call and
holds no read transaction across calls (each Durable Object request is its own
transaction), so a single server-side snapshot of the tasks table is not
available; its documented consistent-read contract for paged readers is "keep
the head of your first page and resume the feed from it: rows that change while
it pages are replayed there" (worker/src/board.ts snapshot()). The replay
already covered status. It now also covers membership: a card whose FIRST
in-window change is the Board's creation row (full row, `version` 1, which
Tx.put writes only on INSERT; `id` equal to the uid; a status) joins the
inventory. Open at the final head, it must carry an origin string and an
integer priority <= PRIORITY_CAP (the apply-time arrival rule in
kanban_priority_inventory.classify); terminal there it stays out of scope.

Still refused: a card missing from the snapshot whose first in-window change is
not a creation row (it existed before the first head, or a patch-shaped
upsert); a new open card above the cap or without an origin; any id, origin or
priority change on a snapshot row or a created row (also when it ends
terminal); any delete. A priority change on a page read before the change
leaves the snapshot stale and refuses; one on a page read after it is already
the fixed-head value, so the capture is exact (tested both ways).

Why not a server-side fixed-head snapshot: it would need a new Board endpoint
(one-call projection of id/status/created_on/priority) in hermes-hub, a
Cloudflare deploy and a client fallback for old Boards, and could not replace
the feed check for verify. Reconstructing as-of-head from the change log is not
possible either: sweep() compacts changes below the floor, so a long-unchanged
row edited mid-window has no older version left. The feed replay is complete
and dense from the first head, so first-head snapshot + replay IS a fixed-head
read; this change finishes it instead of adding a second mechanism.

Five whys (why did three PRs each meet a new race?):
1. Capture refused: a card created mid-window was missing from its page.
2. Why was that a refusal? check_interval treated the replay as a validator of
   a snapshot assumed to be static, refusing whatever it could not explain,
   instead of as the means of moving the snapshot to one head.
3. Why validate instead of reconstruct? #1947 and #1975 each fixed the one
   shape seen in a live refusal (status flips, page-head order) by widening
   what the validator tolerates, so the window design was never stated as
   "the result is the inventory at head H".
4. Why did review not catch it? Each review checked the PR's claim against
   hand-built fixtures with two pages and one change; none had creations,
   completions and reopenings spread across every page with an exact
   ground-truth comparison, so the remaining race classes were invisible.
5. Why no such fixture? The tests asserted refusals and acceptances, not the
   invariant "capture == board state at the final head". Fix: the new seeded
   BusyBoard simulation asserts exactly that invariant on 20 seeds (all 20
   refuse on main), plus verify read-back through churn on 20 seeds and a real
   mid-window priority change refusing on 20 seeds.

Proof:
- tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_priority_fixed_head.py: 90+ cases. RED on
  origin/main: every busy-board capture/verify seed and every mid-window
  creation case fails ("canonical inventory changed"). GREEN here.
- All tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_priority_*.py (20 files, 401 tests) pass,
  including the #1947/#1975 window-churn, first-head and page-head suites.
- 14 hand mutants of the new conditions (version type/value, id, status,
  first-change, inventory membership, terminal skip, origin type/emptiness,
  priority type/cap, append, sort, inventory insertion): all killed.
- No live priority operation was run.
